# Grants rw access to any GameSir (idVendor 3537) USB device, so the GUI/
# tray app (and CLI) can run as a normal user instead of root.
#
# Blanket-VID rather than a per-PID whitelist deliberately, matching how
# device detection itself works: pyg7 scans any 0x3537 device and
# classifies it structurally (see constants.py's VID, and PROTOCOL.md
# "Device identities") rather than matching a fixed PID list, specifically
# so a brand-new G7-family variant works the moment it's plugged in, no
# constants.py addition or community bug report needed first. Until
# 2026-09-02 this file listed each known PID individually -- correct for
# every variant confirmed so far, but it silently reintroduced the exact
# problem the detection code was redesigned to eliminate: a genuinely new
# variant would be recognized fine by pyg7 and still denied raw USB access
# until someone found the new PID and hand-added a line here. See
# ROADMAP.md item 51.
#
# Uses systemd-logind's uaccess mechanism (TAG+="uaccess") rather than a
# dedicated group -- access is granted automatically to whoever's logged in
# at the physical seat, no group/usermod/re-login setup step needed. This
# is the same mechanism stock rules already use for USB mice, cameras, and
# scanners (see /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-uaccess.rules on most distros).
#
# Scope, worth being explicit about: 3537 is registered to GameSir as a
# manufacturer, not to the G7 Pro specifically -- this grants the same
# local-seated-user access to any GameSir USB device, not only G7 Pro
# controllers/dongles, if a future product ever shares the VID. Same trust
# model as the mice/camera rules above (a physically-present logged-in
# user, not a privilege escalation), just a wider device scope than the
# six identities this project has actually reverse-engineered.
#
# Install: see README.md "Running without root" for setup steps.
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="3537", TAG+="uaccess"
